  "commit": "fcda9d1d7d48d360a7f4b964a2d54d5140521ca7",
  "description": "THE ITERATION AN ADVERSARIAL CONSULTATION PAID FOR. run/0021 closed on its own ledger pass; this run is everything that followed from asking a Fable agent to attack the belief that the remaining v1 work is entirely owner-gated. Four of its findings were verified against the tree first-hand before anything was touched, and three of the four were worse than the report said.\n\nWHAT THE MERGES ARE. #374 rewrote the operations runbook's Trash section, which told an operator the opposite of the code on the one irreversible operation in the product: trash purge refuses BECAUSE a period is archived, where period purge REQUIRES it, there is no retention window at that door at all, and the directory purge the page described does not exist. #376 gave Run.merges a field pair per main-push workflow and a guard that refuses if the set drifts. #378 deleted a directory literally named `undefined` that had been shipping 16,681 unreferenced bytes into every image layer, and made the class refuse. #379 corrected four texts the tree contradicts, two of them worse than reported.\n\nTHE RUN BOUNDARY IS ITSELF A FINDING, recorded in run/0021's closing note: nothing triggers it, so it drifts open until somebody decides. This run is opened deliberately rather than inherited, and startTime is the loop trigger's own firing time (2026-09-21T20:44:58Z) rather than a round number -- 23 seconds after run/0021's close, which is also what makes it strictly after, as the validator requires.\n\nMY OWN TWO MISTAKES THIS RUN, both caught by CI on #376 and both worth the space. I inserted a constant BETWEEN checkMergeEntry()'s docblock and its signature, orphaning the `@param list<string>` and costing phpstan the by-ref type at the call site. And I rewrote two blocks that .github/mutations.json registers by exact source text, which mutation-check caught and named precisely. The second was repointed, not weakened: same expectFailing tests, and each new `remove` text was applied to a copy of the validator and shown to silence it -- because \"I updated the registry to match\" is the sentence a weakened guard would also produce.\n\nTHE CLAUSE SWEEP LOOP STEP 4 MANDATES, RUN IN FULL AND REPORTED EMPTY. Every clause of every open v1 item -- 682 lines across six items, eligible or not -- read rather than summarised, because the item-granularity reading has been wrong seven times in this project and the previous iteration reported \"all owner-gated\" at exactly that granularity. It holds at clause granularity too: item/release-v0-1-0's three proofs, item/image-size's \"printed by the release job\", item/readme-owner's clause 2, item/v1-audit's clause (c) subject and item/tag-v1-0-0's clauses (1)(2)(3) all need one tag push; item/docs-site needs two repository settings. Nothing buildable was found, which is what makes a ledger-only pull request permitted here.\n\nTHE SWEEP PAID FOR ITSELF ANYWAY, twice. item/tag-v1-0-0's clause (1) records that mutation/0057 exists to prove widening assert-release-gate.py's edge list did not weaken the edge decision/0120 put there -- and PR #366 widened it again, with two more edges and a gate-equality rule, without re-running that mutant. Re-run here: dropping boot-published-image still exits 1, and so does dropping image -> changelog-section. The guarantee survives, now checked rather than inherited. And the sweep turned up that every release.yml line citation in the ledger had rotted, which is this pass's other half.\n\nTHE COMPOSE STACK BOOTED FOR THE FIRST TIME, 2026-09-22. Every `docker compose` invocation CI had ever run was `config`, which renders and interpolates the file and starts nothing -- so compose-secret proved the secret is required and proved nothing about whether the stack runs, while CLAUDE.md calls `docker compose up -d --build` \"the real stack\" and quick-start.md offers it as a first-class install path.\n\nI ALSO OVER-BLOCKED MYSELF ON IT, one iteration earlier, and the correction is the reusable part. I reported the whole item blocked on issue #382, having conflated what the DOCS should say (the owner's) with what CI should ASSERT (mine). Booting the default stack and asserting the app answers needs no product decision at all; only the sibling-MinIO half does. That is LOOP step 6's standing lesson one level up -- a refused command establishes that THAT command was refused, and an open decision establishes that THAT decision is open, not that everything touching it is blocked.\n\nMAIN WENT RED AT 7fa7fb5 AND WAS GREEN AGAIN AT 71cd528, one cycle later. The cause was the compose-boot job added an hour earlier, and it was neither the job nor a flake: all four default-stack services mount the same db: volume at /data, `docker compose up` starts them within a millisecond of each other, and two race on `mkdir /data/caddy` -- the loser fails to start with `file exists`. The PR run won that race and main's run lost it. Issue #385 carries the log.\n\nTHE RED MERGE IS RECORDED AS `failure` rather than smoothed over. Rule 3 requires only the run's LAST merge to close green, which is what makes an honest red in the middle recordable at all.\n\nWHAT THE INCIDENT IS EVIDENCE FOR, stated because the easy reading is the wrong one: not that the job was bad, but that the compose path had never been executed and therefore had an unobserved race in it. CLAUDE.md's \"several bugs in this codebase were invisible to a fully green suite\" is the whole argument, and this is an instance of it arriving on schedule. What the job cost was one red main for eleven minutes; what it bought is a defect in the documented install path that nobody would otherwise have seen until a self-hoster hit it.\n\nTHE RACE WAS DIAGNOSED WRONG THREE TIMES AND FIXED ON THE FOURTH READING, 2026-09-22, in PR #388. The `mkdir /data/caddy: file exists` failure is the DOCKER DAEMON'S, seeding a fresh named volume by copying the image's content at the mount path -- not any entrypoint's. Three of the four default-stack services override `command:` in compose.yaml, so they never run the image's CMD and never start Caddy at all; the configuration has exactly one possible runner of that code and a race needs two. Everything above in this description, in issue #385's body and in the revert commit that says \"each entrypoint creates its runtime directories under /data\" is wrong about the actor, and all of it is mine. decision/0133 is what that cost and what would have caught it: enumerate the actors a configuration permits before assigning one to an error message that names an operation but no caller.\n\nTHE FIX IS THAT THE IMAGE MUST SHIP NOTHING UNDER /data. `rm -rf /data && mkdir -p /data && chown www-data:www-data /data`: it must still exist so a fresh volume inherits its ownership, and empty there is nothing for the daemon to copy. XDG_DATA_HOME is left alone, which also settles the option I had recommended on #385 and had explicitly marked unverified -- whether relocating it would strand anything. Read from source rather than assumed: the base image sets SSL_MODE=off and CADDY_AUTO_HTTPS=off, doccum overrides neither, ssl-mode/off.caddyfile has one plain http site block and imports no tls directive, and caddy.go's InstanceID() regenerates instance.uuid when absent. Nothing durable, and relocating would have been the one option capable of stranding certificates for anyone running SSL_MODE=full.\n\nAND storage.md'S SPEC 13 CLAIM IS VINDICATED, not refuted as #385 suspected: the four containers DO start independently. The claim simply had an unstated requirement, which PR #388 states next to it and CI now enforces.\n\nWHAT PROVES THE FIX IS NOT THE JOB THAT FOUND THE BUG. compose-boot returns to main with the fix, as the removal commit promised, but a green run of a racy path is a lucky run -- this job's first two runs demonstrated exactly that on identical code. The proof is a deterministic step in the `image` job that REFUSES an image shipping content under /data, per decision/0113, shown failing by mutation/0060 with the Dockerfile's `rm -rf /data` taken back out. decision/0134 generalises it: guard the property an intermittent defect depends on, keep the integration test as a witness.\n\nISSUE #382'S OWN PROPOSAL WAS ALSO CHECKED AND FOUND NOT TO WORK, which changes what half two of item/compose-boot-ci is blocked on. Compose does not let the host shell override a literal `environment:` value, so the command block that issue proposes renders `DOCCUM_EMBEDDED_STORAGE: \"true\"` on app and `AWS_ENDPOINT: http://app:9000` on all three workers regardless (Compose v5.1.1, output posted on #382). The real choice is between changing compose.yaml to interpolate those two values and documenting the two-step honestly -- not between two documentation edits, as the issue framed it.",
